<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taishō period</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Taisho</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Taishō period was the era of Japanese history corresponding to the reign of Emperor Taishō, from 1912 to 1926. It is associated with a growth of party politics, parliamentary government and liberal ideas, often called Taishō democracy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genro">genro</a>, an informal council of elder statesmen, provided coherent governance through the Meiji and Taishō periods, but left no institutional successor as military factions gained power in the 1930s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taisho-period.jpg" alt="Taishō period" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Imperial Household Agency · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emperor_Taisho_of_Japan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>On 30 July 1912 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji">Emperor Meiji</a> died and Crown Prince Yoshihito succeeded him. In his coronation address the new emperor announced the era name Taishō, meaning great righteousness, taken from a passage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/i-ching">I Ching</a>. The end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji period</a> had left heavy government and defence spending, nearly exhausted credit and a lack of foreign reserves, and a new intellectual ferment, with growing interest in socialism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> and an urban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a>. The Japan Socialist Party had been dissolved a year after its founding.</p><p>The era opened with the Taishō political crisis of 1912 and 1913. When Saionji Kinmochi tried to cut the military budget, the army minister resigned and the cabinet fell, and the genrō could find no solution. Public anger at military manipulation of the cabinet and at the recall of Katsura Tarō strengthened demands for an end to genrō politics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on 23 August 1914 and quickly occupied German-leased territories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> and the north Pacific islands. In January 1915 it presented the Twenty-One Demands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, which would have reduced China to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>; after widespread anti-Japanese feeling in China and international condemnation Japan withdrew the final group. The war turned Japan from a debtor into a creditor nation. In 1918 more than 70,000 Japanese troops joined the Allied intervention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> riots that summer brought down the cabinet of Terauchi Masatake.</p><p>In 1918 Hara Takashi became the first commoner to serve as prime minister. Students, professors and journalists held large, orderly demonstrations for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-suffrage">universal male suffrage</a> in 1919 and 1920. Hara was assassinated in 1921 and nonparty and coalition cabinets followed. The General Election Law of 1925 gave the vote to men over 25, but the Peace Preservation Law of the same year forbade any change in the political structure or the abolition of private property. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-naval-conference">Washington Naval Conference</a> of 1921 and 1922, which set a 5:5:3 capital ship ratio for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Japan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Great Kantō Earthquake of September 1923 is described as a turning point: martial law gave the military a greater role and was used to suppress leftist movements. The Japanese Communist Party, founded in July 1922, was forced underground by 1926. From 1927 the Seiyūkai and the Rikken Minseitō alternated in power until 1932.</p><p>Parliamentary government was not rooted deeply enough to withstand the pressures of the 1930s, and the ambiguity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution">Meiji Constitution</a> about the emperor's position made the shift to military influence possible. The army's unauthorised invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> in 1931 marked the end of civilian control. Emperor Taishō died on 25 December 1926 and was succeeded by Crown Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirohito">Hirohito</a>, who had been regent since 1921.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taishō era” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The genro, an informal council of elder statesmen such as Ito Hirobumi and Yamagata Aritomo, provided coherent direction through the Meiji and <b>Taisho</b> periods, but as this generation died the system produced no institutional successor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
